Deterministic sampling — what we did
We used deterministic, policy-level sampling to make our model development reproducible and prevent data leakage between training, validation and test datasets.

Each policy was assigned to one of 10,000 buckets using a consistent hash-based approach. We allocated 81% to training, 9% to validation and 10% to testing.

This ensures that all historical snapshots of the same policy remain in the same dataset, rather than appearing across training and testing.

To reduce training cost, we retained all positive cases and sampled 5% of negative cases. Validation and test datasets remained untouched at their natural class distribution.

How we validated it
Check	Result
Policy-level separation	Confirmed no policy overlap between splits
Train / validation / test allocation	81% / 9% / 10%
Training negative sampling	5%, with all positives retained
Validation and test	Full datasets, no class downsampling
Independent holdout evaluation	2.22 million test observations
Model consistency	Validation and test performance closely aligned

Important distinction: Deterministic sampling guarantees repeatable assignments for the same inputs and hashing logic. It does not guarantee identical model results if the underlying source data changes.
One-line takeaway
“We built a reproducible, policy-level sampling framework that prevents cross-split policy leakage, reduces training cost and preserves unbiased validation and test populations.”

One nuance for technical questions: validation and test are representative of the original population with respect to sampling, but they are not time-based holdouts. An out-of-time validation would be a separate future check.

RFC Model Calibration — 3 key points
1. Why calibration was needed: We retained all positive cases but sampled only 5% of negatives for training. This inflated the model's predicted probabilities relative to the actual population.

2. What we did: Applied a mathematical prior-probability correction using the known 5% negative sampling rate, without retraining the model.

3. How we validated it: On the independent test dataset:
- Average predicted probability reduced from 35.71% to 3.05%, close to the actual review rate of 3.18%.
- Brier score improved from 0.1479 to 0.0301 (lower is better).
- Ranking performance and top-K lift remained unchanged.
Key takeaway for your team lead:
We corrected the probability distortion introduced by training downsampling, bringing predicted probabilities much closer to actual customer review rates while preserving the model's ability to rank customers.

Important: Overall calibration improved substantially, although some differences remain across risk segments.
